  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the 15 files required to plan this refactor, spanning all three repos in dependency order:\n\n**spring-kafka** (primary implementation): `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer.java`, `KafkaMessageListenerContainer.java`, `AbstractMessageListenerContainer.java`, `MessageListenerContainer.java`, `ContainerProperties.java`, `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory.java`, `AbstractKafkaListenerContainerFactory.java`\n\n**spring-boot** (autoconfiguration consumer): `KafkaProperties.java`, `KafkaAutoConfiguration.java`, `KafkaAnnotationDrivenConfiguration.java`, `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer.java`\n\n**spring-framework** (core abstractions): `AsyncTaskExecutor.java`, `TaskExecutor.java`, `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor.java`, `SmartLifecycle.java`\n\nKey finding relevant to the plan: `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer` already exists in spring-kafka 3.0.9 as a composite that spawns N `KafkaMessageListenerContainer` instances (one per `setConcurrency(int)`), each getting its own `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor`. `AbstractMessageListenerContainer` (from spring-framework's `SmartLifecycle`/`AsyncTaskExecutor` abstractions) is the shared base both containers extend. Boot's `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer` already wires `spring.kafka.listener.concurrency` from `KafkaProperties.Listener` into the factory. This means the target architecture is largely already in place \u2014 the refactor is about confirming/extending this existing threading model rather than introducing it from scratch.",
